{"nodes":[{"id":"doc_code_conformance","label":"Code-level conformance audit","file_type":"document","source_file":"docs/CODE-CONFORMANCE.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Records where the fleet stands against code-level standards; fixes nothing so each finding stays a separate decision taken with the measurement in hand."},{"id":"concept_code_audit","label":"Code-level conformance audit","file_type":"document","source_file":"docs/CODE-CONFORMANCE.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"37 findings over 122 audited rule clusters; a rule violated in 400 files is one row with a count, not four hundred rows.","entity_type":"concept"},{"id":"concept_finding_ranks","label":"Finding ranks and the security flag","file_type":"document","source_file":"docs/CODE-CONFORMANCE.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Four ranks describe realized consequence — breaks a consumer, breaks a promise, unenforced, style only. Security is a flag rather than a fifth rank because a supply-chain exposure is risk, and unenforced is already the risk bucket.","entity_type":"concept"},{"id":"concept_audit_classes","label":"Audit sliced by catching mechanism","file_type":"document","source_file":"docs/CODE-CONFORMANCE.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Classes are Boundary, CI, Arch, Larastan, Pint and Prose; Prose (per-change) is out of scope because an obligation on the next change has no fleet-wide fact behind it and an audit row for one cannot fail.","entity_type":"concept"},{"id":"finding_livewire_aliases","label":"Livewire aliases are not package-qualified","file_type":"document","source_file":"docs/CODE-CONFORMANCE.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contribut